README-NOT-SHIPPED -- programs found in the archives that stayed outPrograms you might look for and not find, why each is not here, andwhere it is.  Duplicates, fragments and programs that cannot run are notlisted.  Archive names are those of the Microware OS-9 archive and theEFFO forum disks; SOURCES.txt quotes each program's terms in full.THE AUTHOR'S TERMS DO NOT ALLOW A DISK LIKE THIS------------------------------------------------  vi          An ex/vi for OS-9/68000.  Its first line says it              "originates from the sources of vi running under the XENIX              operating system", and nobody who could grant it has.  Its              helpers stay with it: expreserve and exrecover (save and              restore a buffer when vi dies), add_errmsg, read_mail, and              SYS/vi_usage, SYS/vi_errmsg and SYS/.exrc.  EFFO forum disk              13, SOFTWARE/C/VI.  The vi here is PVIC, public domain;              DOC/README-VI.  RCS 4       ci, co, rcs, rcsdiff, rcsident, rcsmerge, rlog.  Its READ_ME              is Purdue's non-disclosure form: "RCS will not be              distributed in any form or by any means without prior              written permission".  CMDS/rcs4.lha.  RCS 5 and later are              GNU-licensed.  sysmon      A system monitor.  Its source is marked "the proprietary              confidential property of MAX PLANCK INSTITUTE KERNPHYSIK              HEIDELBERG"; the author offers it freely, but the notice is              the institute's.  CMDS/SYSMON.lzh.  SEDT        An editor (e, new_e), available "for customers and Digital              internal use on the condition that ... no modifications are              made".  EFFO forum disk 11, SOFTWARE/C/SEDT_EDITOR.  btree, isam Softfocus BTREE and ISAM.  The forum disk calls them public              domain; their btree.doc says they no longer are, and sells              them for about $65.  EFFO forum disk 2.  tshell      A shell: "NOT Public Domain, but its free for private use".              EFFO public-domain disk 4.  rz, sz      Omen Technology's ZMODEM, $20 a user by the order form in              the archive.  APPS/rzsz_3_36_3_OSK.lzh.  k, xy and z in              CMDS/COMMS are a free X/Y/ZMODEM.  smbfm       An SMB (Samba) client, free to copy, but "No part of this              material may be distributed with other software packages              without the express permission from the author".              NETWORK/smbfm14t.zip.  greed       "Please don't redistribute this."  A 1989 Usenet posting,              from v_misc.ar on the hc disk.  travesty    A text generator whose permission ran "Until January 1,              1994".  From Usenet.  ape does the same job here.  puzzle15, puz15, udate, uwho              Unmodified copies only, and an OS-9 build is a modified              copy.  EFFO forum disk 16 (puzzle15), v_misc.ar on the hc              disk (puz15), the UNaXcess BBS (udate, uwho).MICROWARE'S OWN---------------Your OS-9 has these.  The collection carries only the six run-timemodules Microware gave permission for (DOC/README-CIO, DOC/fpu.doc).  break       The system-debugger utility, disassembled.  EFFO forum              disk 5.  msfm        MS-DOS file manager source from Dibble's OS-9 Insights,              carrying Microware's notice.  EFFO forum disk 12.  cio020, fpu040              Run-time modules found inside other programs' archives.  load        A clean-room rewrite of Microware's utility, from the              published manuals.  A program named for one of Microware's              is theirs to ship.  Use your own `load'; SYS/login puts              /h1/CMDS on PATH.FOUND, BUT THEY DO NOT WORK WITH ANYTHING HERE----------------------------------------------  pbmexec     Fifteen early PBM tools (pbmtops, pbmcrop, cbmtopbm ...).              They reject PBM files that netpbm, in CMDS/NETPBM, reads              and writes correctly.